    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hearing loss is a pertinent issue affecting over 5% of the world’s population, or more than 450 million people. Unlike mammals, avians regenerate their hearing upon damage. Consequently, establishing avians as a model organism for mammalian hearing regeneration has immense therapeutic and research value. To do so, the evolutionary relationship between the inner ears of mammals and avians must be established. Current research by Manley and Köppl suggests that the homology between the avian and mammalian inner ears is limited to an ancestral “hair cell field”, or basilar papilla, with uniform hair-cell types. They claim other specializations, such as distinct hair cell types, arose independently in mammals and avians and are not homologous. \r\n\r\nThe aim of this study is to determine whether there is support for homology, or common evolutionary origin, between modern avian and mammalian inner ears. Specifically, this research comprises two parts. First, it seeks to establish similarity relationships between the two major mammalian and avian hair cell subtypes. Establishing similarity between these cell groups could yield support for homology between modern avian and mammalian inner ears. Second, it seeks to use BLAST protein alignment to resolve the annotation challenges associated with cross-species gene alignment. Rather than comparing gene names, which vary between species, this research directly compares protein sequences, yielding improved alignment outcomes. Hopefully, the techniques used here can be applied by others conducting cross-species gene alignments. Ultimately, the study will allow our understanding of avian hearing regeneration to inform mammalian regenerative therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
